+Agents degrade in production, and unlike pre-release evaluation the response
+has no standard form. We present the Agent Performance Improvement Plan (APIP),
+which adapts the management protocol for underperforming employees into a
+remediation lifecycle for agents: statistically disciplined triggers on a
+behavioral contract, cause attribution over a five-category failure-mode
+taxonomy, tiered interventions, a bounded window with formal exit criteria, and
+a declarative record that meets governance traceability requirements. We
+evaluate it end-to-end in two live multi-agent environments ($\tau^2$-bench and
+ChatDev) by injecting failure modes with known ground truth and scoring against
+no-action, always-Tier-1, and oracle baselines. Under stale-corpus drift, only
+APIP and the oracle exit durably; the always-Tier-1 responder patches four
+times, regresses three times, and ends indistinguishable from no action, so
+time-to-resolution without a durability criterion rewards the worst strategy.
+Attribution is correct in all three non-abstaining cycles. A null result on
+mesh insertion suggests that mesh disruption requires a bounded shared context.
 \end{abstract}
 
